          "question": "What is coronary artery disease and how does it develop over time?",
          "answer": "Coronary artery disease (CAD) is the build-up of plaque in the arteries that supply blood to the heart muscle. According to the general clinical reference \"Coronary artery disease,\" plaque builds up over decades. It starts with injury to the inner lining of the artery and a gradual collection of cholesterol-rich material beneath it.\n\nThe same source says two main mechanisms cause symptoms:\n\n- **Gradual narrowing.** The artery narrows slowly, so it cannot supply enough blood when the heart works harder. This typically causes predictable chest discomfort during exertion, called stable angina.\n- **Plaque rupture.** A plaque can crack, which triggers a blood clot that can suddenly block the artery. This is an acute coronary syndrome, such as a heart attack. It can happen at a plaque that was never narrow enough to cause symptoms. So a normal stress test in the past does not rule out a heart attack now.\n\nThe ICD-10-CM coding reference places coronary artery disease within the \"ischemic heart disease\" group. It runs from angina through acute heart attack to the chronic disease that follows.\n\nThe secondary prevention guidance from major cardiology societies describes measures for people who already have established CAD. These include antiplatelet medicines and statins, along with other measures. The passages I retrieved do not describe those measures in detail, so I cannot go further on them here.\n\nThe retrieved passages did not cover what causes plaque to form in the first place, or the symptoms of each stage in detail. A clinician can explain how this applies to a specific person.\n\n**Seek emergency care now** if chest pain or pressure is severe, lasts more than a few minutes, or comes with breathlessness, sweating, nausea, or pain spreading to the arm, jaw, or back. Call your local emergency number. Do not drive yourself.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
